data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` into `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** Both work exactly as you'd expect:

```csharp
Span<nint>  signed   = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> unsigned = stackalloc nuint[4];
```

On this 64-bit system each element is 8 bytes; on a 32-bit runtime they'd be 4 bytes. The only gotcha is that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context since the size isn't fixed at compile time — but `stackalloc` itself works fine in safe code when assigned to a `Span`.
